WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:16.850
When you first start looking through a microscope
at microorganisms, it’s very obvious that

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.180
each little cell is its own thing.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.310
But then, as time goes on, and you start to
understand their relationships and their dependence

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:33.370
on each other, and also everything’s dependence
on them, each microbe starts to feel like

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:39.719
a piece of some much broader, even global
organism.

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:44.840
And of course, species of all kinds and sizes
have their ecological impacts.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:50.980
And this actually becomes much more obvious
when you make that ecosystem less global by,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.390
say, putting it in a little box.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:58.540
And you might have seen one of these boxes
recently...maybe in your home, or at a restaurant,

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.600
or the doctor's office.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.989
We are talking, of course, about aquariums.

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.890
This tank is just one of those tanks at a
mall aquarium store.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:13.640
It's got some reefs and some fishes swimming
around, living in full display for people

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.170
passing by.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.999
But just give it a moment...just a few seconds.

00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:21.759
And there.

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:27.470
That is the disembodied hand of an aquarium
shop worker who probably went to work expecting

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:34.740
the usual aquarium shop business, until James,
our master of microscopes, came in and asked

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.639
if he could please have some water from the
tank.

00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:44.450
Science is always a team effort, and fortunately,
this shop worker was a willing collaborator,

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:50.840
searching around for biofilm and other bits
from the tank in response to various requests

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.020
thrown his way.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.920
Of course, there are plenty of macroscopic
animals in the aquarium to admire.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.420
Have you ever wanted to see what a starfish
looks like when you're really up close?

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.420
Yeah. I mean, of course you have.

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:08.149
But the real focus of this expedition wasn’t
the fishes or the large invertebrates that

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:11.150
we can clearly see with our own eyes.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:16.120
What we want to see is what else is in that tank.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.530
This question is actually pretty important
for any aquarium you might want to set up

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.530
at home.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.750
Sure, you might be most excited about the
fish.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:30.950
But fish waste is full of ammonia, which can
accumulate to toxic levels if left unchecked.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:37.150
In nature, microbes play an essential role
in the nitrogen cycle, including taking extremely

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:43.450
stable atmospheric nitrogen and turning it
into nutrients like these Trichodesmium do,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.780
or by consuming nitrogenous waste.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:52.200
To make sure that aquatic ecosystems have
the same cleaning abilities built in, people

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:59.010
install bio filters that contain nitrogen-consuming
bacteria in their aquariums.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:05.470
It’s sort of like building wastewater treatment
into the artificial ecological neighborhood.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:10.430
And beyond the basic care of keeping tanks,
scientists have been studying the microbial

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:14.820
composition of aquariums to explore all sorts
of questions.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:20.600
One group collected tank water from seven
Rhode Island pet shops to examine the diversity

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:25.750
of microbial communities between them, finding
potential sources of pathogens that might

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:27.730
require further study.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:34.410
Another group observed that freshwater aquarium
bio filters are dominated more by ammonia-oxidizing

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.260
archaea than they are by bacteria.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.360
There are, of course, also large aquariums.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.450
Like very large.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.430
So large you have to drive out to visit them
so you can stand in awe.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:54.730
And yes, scientists have been studying the
microbiomes of these large tanks too.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:04:01.310
This includes a study of the Ocean Voyager
exhibit in Georgia, one of the largest aquariums

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:07.590
in the United States, where 14 months of tracking
the microbial community of the exhibit revealed

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:14.319
a surprising dynamic and changing community
despite the relative stability of the water

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:15.490
itself.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:23.570
Just for reference, the Ocean Voyager exhibit
contains about 6.3 million gallons of artificial

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:30.280
seawater, and it houses very cool, odd animals
including the blacktip reef shark, the longcomb

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:33.120
sawfish, and the porcupine ray.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:40.080
In contrast, the mall aquarium has a lot less
water in it, and the fish are not quite as varied.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.840
But fortunately, we don’t need millions
of gallons of water

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.960
to find fascinating ecological diversity

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.360
We just need a microscope.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:55.909
So we were pretty impatient to see what was
in that carefully obtained, mall tank water.

00:04:55.909 --> 00:05:00.610
One of the first organisms that caught our
attention was foraminifera, this kind of conch

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.260
shell-looking thing.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:07.020
Foraminifera, or forams for short,
are protists, and they can be found living

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:12.990
all over the ocean, both in terms of depth
and geographical location.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:19.379
There are around 4,000 species of forams around
the world, of which we found only a handful

00:05:19.379 --> 00:05:21.090
in this sample.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.960
Their shells come in different shapes and
are made up of different material depending

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.759
on what species they are.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:33.020
Now forams may look innocuous, but they've
been around for over 500 million years.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:38.900
And with all those forams living and dying
over that long period, their shells have built

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:45.479
up on the deep ocean floor, accumulating to
the point where in death, they’re practically

00:05:45.479 --> 00:05:47.490
a geological entity.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:52.990
In turn, the chemical composition of their
shells has helped us better understand how

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:59.860
our oceans and climate have changed over millions
of years.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:04.669
But while we were watching our new foram friends,
something else came along.

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:11.089
You can see it here, that long, thin line
snaking between the magnified sand grains.

00:06:11.089 --> 00:06:16.349
It looks like a worm, but we think it’s
actually a Tracheloraphis, and I do not know

00:06:16.349 --> 00:06:20.939
if we’re pronouncing that correctly, a mysterious
single-celled organism.

00:06:20.939 --> 00:06:26.030
It's difficult to find a lot of information
on Tracheloraphis, probably because it’s

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:30.070
been difficult to culture in a lab for further
study.

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:32.580
Even filming it turned out to be a challenge.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.930
The slide we were watching it on had too much
sand and debris in it for us to get a good

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:37.930
recording.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:43.539
So we tried moving it over to a new slide,
taking off the coverslip and using a micropipette

00:06:43.539 --> 00:06:45.070
to grab it.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:51.289
Except that once we removed the coverslip,
that little guy just disappeared!

00:06:51.289 --> 00:06:57.040
It took 3 hours to find it and prepare it
in this sample, but it was worth it for this

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:03.280
close up view of just how weird this unicellular
organism is.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.320
What else did we find?

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:15.469
An amoeba spreading against microbial fauna
as its own internal crystals sparkle, like

00:07:15.469 --> 00:07:19.180
an opening in tree branches that give way
to a galaxy.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:27.151
There also long cyanobacteria stalks, the
idling dinoflagellates, the stunning trichodesmium

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:33.509
filaments, and this ciliate who seemed to
not quite know what it wanted to be doing.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.980
I know that feel, buddy.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:43.189
And all of this from some scoops taken from
a mall aquarium...all of these organisms make

00:07:43.189 --> 00:07:50.930
those clear boxes of glass not just a storage
tank for some pretty fish, but a living ecosystem

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:57.379
with millions of organisms all depending on
each other to turn the basics of chemistry

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.240
into the majesty of life.

00:08:05.409 --> 00:08:10.969
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:10.969 --> 00:08:14.660
And if you like this show, who you really
want to thank are these people whose names

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:21.309
are on the screen right now, our patrons on
Patreon who make it possible for us to search

00:08:21.309 --> 00:08:29.590
through grains of sand for hours so that we
can get a good picture of a weird micobe.

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:34.930
Thank you for indulging with us and allowing
us to indulge in this curiosity.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:39.960
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James, check out Jam &amp; Germs on

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:40.960
Instagram

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.940
And if you want to see more from us, you can
find us at youtube.com/microcosmos

